# The following code solves Problem 5c on HW#2. 
# In particular, if you save this code under the name 
# easy5c.map, run maple, then say 
#  read("easy5c.map"); 
# maple should say 
#  104, 116, 116, 112, 115, 58, 47, 47, 119, 119, 119, 46, 121, 111, 117, 116,
#   117, 98, 101, 46, 99, 111, 109, 47, 119, 97, 116, 99, 104, 63, 118, 61, 
#   45, 79, 80, 67, 74, 89, 79, 106, 74, 50, 111
# If you then copy these numbers into an array in matlab and (in matlab) 
# say 
#  nums=[104, 116, 116, 112, 115, 58, 47, 47, 119, 119, 119, 46, 121, 111, 117, 116, 117, 98, 101, 46, 99, 111, 109, 47, 119, 97, 116, 99, 104, 63, 118, 61, 45, 79, 80, 67, 74, 89, 79, 106, 74, 50, 111]; 
# and then say 
#  char(nums) 
# you should get: 
#  https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-OPCJYOjJ2o 
# which should lead you to a video of my friends, Choro das Tres...

n:=15565728732977275987537732464288335871582590656780416209943917687896713120752382885278924529477398852712: 
s[1]:=(n mod 256): 
m[1]:=floor(n/256): 
for i from 2 to 43 do 
 s[i]:=(m[i-1] mod 256): 
 m[i]:=floor(m[i-1]/256): 
end:
print(seq(s[k],k=1..43));
